Z||000000||bHooper||02-23-2000||11:17 PM||||there is a '86 spd automatic for sale, locally.  the lady says that the tranny keeps leaking fluid, saying it's the transmision seal.  any thoughts on how difficult this would be to have fixed? cost?  the car is gold, with a sunroof and might be a 4 cyl SE!  she doesn't really know, she said it did have a rounded front end, but was listed on her insurance as a coupe.  she was listing the car for $1000, but when i talked with her she said that she would take $500... just to get rid of it.  <P>what do you guys think about the tranny?  i will look at the car, even though it's not exactly what i want... but if i could get it cheap enough and fix it, i could probably make a little on the car.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Phaeton||02-23-2000||11:26 PM||||Was it run until it slipped and then fluid added? how many times?<BR>If the car is worth a thousand then the five hundred you save is enough to fix the tranny. Unless the take out and put back have to be paid for retail.<BR>Any local mechs handy to have a look and listen with you, nothing beats being there.||||reg||1
Z||000002||batboy||02-23-2000||11:28 PM||||If she's talking about the seal where the axles come out, it's like replacing CV joints, not too bad to do. Ogre had a description on this procedure in a past thread. If she means the seal up by the  torque convertor, you have to drop the tranny, which is considerably more work. It's almost easier to drop the whole cradle assembly.||||reg||1
Z||000003||bHooper||02-23-2000||11:36 PM||||i'll find out for sure tommorow.  if i am the least bit interested, i will take it somewhere to be looked at!  <P>of course, it's always great to have your thoughts on it, as i know nothing about the automatics!<P>it's sometimes better to have a whole lot of knowledge, when looking at a <B>cheap</B> car... especially one that droped $500 in a day!||||reg||1
Z||000004||bHooper||02-24-2000||01:51 PM||||well i've looked at the car... and the transmision fluid levels are <B>full</B>... go figure!  it seems that the problem began with a leaking valve cover, first noticed on 11/18/99 when the oil was changed.  but 400 miles and 3 months later, there is no oil in the pan what-so-ever!!!!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><P>well, maam... sorry, maam... i... i... i... really dont know what to tell you about your car...  i... i... i'll have to think about it, maam....<P>thinking done, i dont need another "project" car at the moment!<P>but, if any of you are interested........||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||02-24-2000||07:35 PM||||sounds like someone didn't do the oil change right. A valve cover would have to leak really bad and probly cause a fire to lose that much oil.||||reg||1
Z||000006||bHooper||02-25-2000||07:15 AM||||normally, i would agree.  however, i think she had the leaky valve cover to begin with, but followed it up by blowing the head gasket!  that would explain all the oil on everything, and why the car didnt want to turn over.  <P>i have a feeloing, i will wee this car in one of the salvage yards soon!||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||02-25-2000||08:42 AM||||If they didn't put the filter on right it could do that to. Either way the motor is toast. I'd buy it for parts maybe.||||reg||1
